{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nDiscovered while fixing bug `c49a-225d-2bc0-4478` (`perplexed-jeanlike-kakarikis`).\n\n`check_wall_clock_asserts` did not flag `test_narrow_operation_persists_the_same_bytes_as_the_legacy_sequence`, which flaked on a wall-clock boundary. The flake mechanism is indirect: the test never asserts on a timestamp directly. Instead it asserts byte-equality between two files independently produced by `BindingStore.save()`, and each save stamps `created_at` / `updated_at` from the wall clock in product code (`BindingLifecycle.bind_confirm` -> `_now_iso`). When the two builds straddle a one-second boundary the serialized bytes differ.\n\nThis is a gate blind spot: a byte/text-equality assertion between separately produced artifacts can be a latent wall-clock flake even when no direct timestamp or elapsed-time assertion appears in the test.\n\n## Prior review finding to address\n\nA prior plan-review BLOCK correctly found that a blanket rule for all cross-artifact `read_bytes()` / `read_text()` equality would create false positives in `make verify`: not every byte-identical artifact comparison embeds clock-derived fields, and the earlier plan had no escape for proven timestamp-free artifacts.\n\n## Current false-positive survey\n\nA repository survey of direct `read_bytes()` / `read_text()` equality comparisons under `tests/**` found these current cases:\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_rich_reemit_state.py`: compares independently saved binding stores whose JSON embeds `created_at` / `updated_at`; this is the motivating timestamp flake and is already fixed by freezing `_now_iso`.\n- `tests/integration/test_comment_echo_reclaim_manifest.py`: compares two manifest outputs and also asserts parsed semantic fields; deterministic generated JSON, no product clock seam identified.\n- `tests/unit/test_fixture_selection_corpus.py`: compares two manifest outputs from identical corpus inputs; deterministic sorted JSONL, no product clock seam identified.\n- `tests/unit/test_build_provenance_fixture_scale.py`: compares fixture materialization to an existing repository file; same source bytes copied, no independently timestamped artifact.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/fetch/test_fetcher_snapshot.py`: compares two snapshots produced from identical stub data; deterministic JSON fixture, no product clock seam identified.\n- `tests/unit/test_fixture_selection_escape_index.py`: compares eager vs batched JSONL over the same corpus; deterministic generated JSONL, no product clock seam identified.\n- `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py`: compares repeated generator output; deterministic golden generation, no product clock seam identified.\n- `tests/unit/test_skill_shared_sync.py`: helper compares a shipped copy to a canonical file; same source bytes, no independently timestamped artifact.\n\nConclusion: the checker should add a conservative proxy rule, but it must include a narrow, reason-required local escape for intentional timestamp-free byte/text equality. That mirrors the existing hang-guard escape pattern: the lint remains fail-closed for unreviewed risky comparisons, while reviewed deterministic comparisons can document why no production clock seam is involved.\n\n\n## Variable-assignment false-positive survey\n\nA second survey for the variable-mediated shape found no current cross-receiver variable comparisons under `tests/**`. The current variable-based byte/text comparisons are same-receiver re-read/idempotency checks and must be exempt by structural receiver equality, including variable-vs-direct forms where the variable was assigned from the same receiver expression:\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_lifecycle.py`: `before = (.../\"bindings.json\").read_bytes()` followed by the same path expression's `read_bytes()` compared to `before`.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_baseline.py`: repeated same-file before/after `read_bytes()` idempotency checks.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_repository.py`: repeated same-file before/after `read_bytes()` idempotency checks.\n- `tests/integration/rebar_reconciler/test_reconcile_idempotency.py`: same output path read into a variable and compared after rerun.\n- `tests/unit/test_complexity_baseline.py`: same baseline/golden path bytes captured and compared after a check.\n- `tests/unit/test_unique_cache_writes.py`: same cache file bytes captured and compared after a second operation.\n\nConclusion: implement variable tracking only within a single test function, record each variable's file-read receiver expression, and treat `var_from_receiver == same_receiver.read_bytes()` / `read_text()` as a same-receiver self-comparison. Only fire when both compared sides resolve to file-read receivers and those receiver expressions are structurally different.\n\n## Decision\n\nOperator-authorized default 2026-09-07: implement a syntactic, conservative proxy rule rather than whole-product timestamp provenance analysis. Refined after the review BLOCK: add a minimal inline escape marker for this new proxy rule so existing and future timestamp-free artifact comparisons do not become false positives.\n\nImplementation scope:\n\n- Extend `scripts/check_wall_clock_asserts.py` to detect unescaped file-content equality/inequality comparisons in test ASTs.\n- Fire on `assert left.read_bytes() == right.read_bytes()` / `!=`, and equivalent `read_text()` comparisons, when the two call receivers are not structurally the same receiver expression.\n- Fire on variables assigned from `read_bytes()` / `read_text()` receivers and later compared for equality/inequality in the same test function only when the resolved receiver expressions are structurally different; variable-vs-direct comparisons to the same receiver are exempt.\n- Do not fire when the enclosing test freezes the production clock seam with `monkeypatch.setattr(..., \"_now_iso\", ...)` or `monkeypatch.setattr(..., \"utc_now_iso\", ...)`.\n- Do not fire when both sides resolve to the same receiver expression (including variable-vs-direct re-reads of the same expression), because that is a deterministic self-comparison rather than independently produced artifacts.\n- Add a narrow inline escape marker, `# timing: artifact-equality — <reason>`, covering the assert line or the line directly above. A reason is mandatory. Use it only when the developer has verified that the compared artifacts are deterministic / timestamp-free or that the comparison is a same-source copy check.\n- Annotate any existing timestamp-free direct comparisons newly flagged by the checker with this marker and a specific reason, while leaving the motivating frozen-clock test unescaped.\n\nThe finding text should explain that the rule is a transitive wall-clock flake proxy and that tests comparing independently produced artifact bytes should freeze the clock, assert semantic fields instead, or use the reason-required escape only for proven timestamp-free artifacts.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Parsing product code to prove whether a compared artifact embeds timestamps.\n- Auditing beyond the current direct `read_bytes()` / `read_text()` equality survey above and the checker's test suite.\n- Adding a broad configuration mechanism, allowlist, or force-style bypass. The only new escape is the local reason-required marker for this specific proxy rule.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_wall_clock_assert_lint.py` has a RED-first test proving the checker flags a direct equality/inequality comparison between two different `read_bytes()` or `read_text()` receivers in a test with no clock freeze and no artifact-equality escape.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_wall_clock_assert_lint.py` has a RED-first test proving the checker flags equality/inequality between variables assigned from different file-content read receivers.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_wall_clock_assert_lint.py` has contrast tests proving same-receiver comparisons, tests that freeze `_now_iso` / `utc_now_iso`, same-receiver variable-vs-direct re-reads, and reason-required `artifact-equality` escapes are not flagged in both documented placements: on the assert line and on the line directly above.\n- [ ] Existing upper-bound wall-clock assertion behavior and hang-guard / CI-skip escapes remain unchanged.\n- [ ] Existing timestamp-free direct comparisons in the survey either remain unflagged by receiver identity/frozen-clock logic or are annotated with a specific reason-required `artifact-equality` escape.\n- [ ] Verification: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest tests/unit/test_wall_clock_assert_lint.py` exits zero, followed before push by `make verify` with the worktree venv on `PATH` and GIT_CONFIG_* unset."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788958301012296001,"uuid":"87c2fe6e-301d-4772-a5e9-6fe5a3d541fc"}